---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

export const title = "Park Butte Fire Lookout";
export const date = "2026-08-28";
export const excerpt =
  "Spending the night in a historic fire lookout in the North Cascades — one of the most memorable nights of the year.";
export const tags = ["hiking", "camping", "north-cascades", "washington"];
const description =
  "Spending the night in a historic fire lookout in the North Cascades, one of the most memorable nights of the year.";
---

<SiteLayout title={`${title} — Saachi K Gupta`} description={description} ogImage="/images/park-butte/lookout-interior-baker-corner.jpg">
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p style="opacity:0.7; font-size: 13px;">{date}</p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Park Butte Lookout sits above the meadows on the shoulder of Mt. Baker, and is a shockingly short and non-steep height for the payoff.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The lookout dates to 1932, built in the wave of fire detection towers that went up across the Cascades during the Great Depression. Park Butte's is one of the few left intact in the range, kept standing today by Skagit Alpine Club volunteers. It's first-come-first-served, no reservation, which is part of why a weekday felt like the move if I wanted to spend the night up there.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Sleeping in a fire lookout had been on my Washington bucket list for a while, and Monday, June 15th, was the day to finally do it: probably my last unemployed weekday in Seattle, and I would be leaving for my family's trip a few days later. I wrote out a detailed itinerary in my Notes app, chatted with a friend who had done the hike a few weeks prior, and felt like I had a solid plan. (More on the days leading up to this one <a href="/reflections/final-weekend-of-funemployment-in-seattle">here</a>.)
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I packed efficiently (not my first rodeo), and made plans for lunch at Google with my friend Owen. My plan was to drive up to Baker after lunch, start hiking by 4, make it to the lookout by 6, and if I wasn't feeling up to staying the night, I could be back at my car by 9 with the last (very marked) mile with headlamps if really required. I was, of course, scared to solo-hike but felt like I had a good plan. I had the route mapped on <a href="https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/washington/park-butte-lookout-trail">AllTrails Pro</a> (7.5 miles round trip, 2,200 feet of gain, by their numbers) with the <a href="https://www.wta.org/go-hiking/hikes/park-butte">WTA page</a> pulled up too. I left Seattle around 1:45, with a 3:45 ETA to the trailhead, drove up Baker Lake Road, turned onto seven miles of gravel, and parked around 4:10pm. Right on plan.
  </p>

