  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    <a href="https://www.strava.com/activities/18951148087">My Strava caption</a> for that one says it best: "2 hrs lookout to car but 10++++ car to home." I wasn't exaggerating enough.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The pothole that wasn't</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I started driving out at 8:11. At 8:23, two miles down the same gravel road I'd come in on, something slammed underneath the car and the tire blew. I told AAA (via satellite texting...) it was a pothole, because that's what it felt like. I had no cell signal out there, only satellite texting, so I sent what I could piece together: the tire had popped, something else had broken, I had no idea what. Then I waited. I was lucky that Baker was busy on this gorgeous Tuesday. Many groups passed me on their way up AND their way down. An Alpine Ascents guide let me use their satellite phone. But I spent over 8 hours alone on a forest road in the North Cascades, with only my (very unreliable) satellite texting.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.8; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.04); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
    4:30a — up for sunrise<br/>
    6a — started hiking down<br/>
    8a — at car, on schedule<br/>
    8:11 — driving out<br/>
    8:23 — something slammed under the car<br/>
    3pm — tow truck
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The tow</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The tow truck picked me up at 3pm for a 98-mile haul from the trailhead to Autologic in Bellevue. My parents had been able to re-schedule my flight. I dropped the car at Autologic that evening. I was physically fine, great even. The car was not.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    While I was sitting in the tow truck cab somewhere on I-5, my Garmin buzzed.
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
