    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 12px 0;"
  />

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/park-butte/tow-truck-tamales.jpg"
    alt="Eating walking tamales in the tow truck on the highway back to Bellevue"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 600px; border-radius: 10px; margin: 0 0 20px 0;"
  />

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">What actually happened</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    In January, I'd had the rear brakes serviced and hadn't thought about it since. I'd only put about 2,000 miles on the car between then and June. At the time, I assumed it was on me: driving too boldly on a potholed gravel road, hitting something wrong. It wasn't until days later, on calls with the shop, that I found out how much more serious it actually was. The left rear brake caliper had come off the car entirely while I was driving through the mountains, and it was the caliper, dangling and dragging, that punctured the tire. The bolts from January's service hadn't been tightened.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">A little of the evening back</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I took the light rail home after, ate takeout, showered, and slept before my morning flight out. It turned out my car and I were back in action in Seattle on the same day, three weeks after the incident.
  </p>

